  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nIt certainly has been a hot stretch in Wyoming through July and \nstarting August. For example, at the humble Riverton Weather Abode, \nof the past 29 days, 28 have averaged above normal. And the one day \nthat was below average, July 23rd, was only 0.2 degrees below \nnormal. We have had 7 high temperatures over 100, we typically \naverage less than 1 a year. And we will almost certainly have \nanother one today. However, there is something moving toward the\narea that will bring a welcome break from one of the hottest \nsummers we have had in quite a while. More on that later in the \ndiscussion.\n\nBut we have one more scorching hot day to get through. We had \nseveral record highs that were broken or tied yesterday, including \nan all time August record high temperature at the Riverton airport. \nIt was also the first time it has been 100 at the airport in the\nmonth of August (since 1996 when the ASOS was commissioned). \nThe 700 millibar temperatures look largely unchanged from \nyesterday, so the high temperatures forecasted for Sunday also \nlook relatively unchanged. With a southwesterly breeze developing,\nthe areas that will be warmer will be the downslope locations. \nThese include the southern Bighorn Basin, the Wind River Basin \nand Lander Foothills and portions of Sweetwater County. We will \nlikely see more daily record high temperatures today. Some all \ntime August high temperatures records could also be in jeopardy.\nIt still looks like elevations below around 5800 feet have at \nleast a 1 in 2 chance of high temperatures over 100F. As for \nhighs of 105F, the 1 in 2 chance is largely below 4500 feet, \nmainly in portions of Fremont County as well the Big Horn Basin \nfrom Thermopolis to Worland. And the gusty breeze will also \nbring increased fire weather concerns. This, combined with \nrelative humidity falling as low as 5 percent, will bring at \nleast elevated fire weather to the entire area. A Red Flag \nWarning is in effect for much of the area, and we will continue \nit. I did give some thought to including Natrona County, but \nstill have some doubts if the wind will be of sufficient \nstrength or duration. Nevertheless, burning is strongly \ndiscouraged anywhere in western and central Wyoming today.\n\nThe relief from the heat for some will come in the form of a cold \nfront dropping southward out of Montana tonight and tomorrow. It \nwill begin to weaken as it moves through the area Monday, but all \nareas will see a drop in temperatures. The most pronounced drops \nwill be across northern Wyoming where places Cody could see \ntemperatures 25 degrees cooler than today with it feeling more like \nSeptember. Most locations East of the Divide will see around a 15 to \n20 degree drop in temperatures, with some locations seeing the \ncoolest temperatures since June. It will also cool off West of the \nDivide, but not as much. However, 8 to 15 degrees will still feel \nbetter. We still have fire weather concerns on this day though. In \nnorthern and central Wyoming, the cooler temperatures and somewhat \nhigher humidity should keep conditions below critical despite a \ngusty northerly breeze. Much of southern Wyoming will still have \nhumidity under 15 percent though. And with the continued wind with \nthe front in the area, critical fire weather still looks likely so \nthe Red Flag Warning will be continued. \n\nTuesday looks like the coolest day across much of the area. There \nwill still be a bit of a breeze and humidity remains low so we may \nneed more fire weather highlights for this day as well although it \nis more borderline. We will have more time to look at that in future \nshifts. Following that, the ridge should again build across the \ndesert southwest and then build back northward into Wyoming. \nThis means temperatures rising again by Wednesday with above \nnormal temperatures again likely from Thursday into next \nweekend, with 90s once again likely in many of the lower \nelevations. As for any precipitation chances, there are rather \nslim pickings. A weak shortwave will move across northern \nWyoming and may bring a few showers and storms Tuesday afternoon\nor evening but with little moisture available coverage looks \nrather sparse. Mainly dry weather then continues for the rest of\nthe week. There are some indications in some of the long range \nguidance of the ridge weakening early next week and maybe some \nmonsoonal moisture returning for early next week but a lot of \nuncertainty this far out. So, enjoy the brief break from the hot\nweather early this week because it will likely not last long.",
